---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../components/content/Callout.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Measuring How Agents Fare on Your Platform"
  description="The Agentic Developer Relations scorecard — six measures, the rules that make them trustworthy, and where to start when you have nothing yet."
>
  <section class="container-site pt-16 pb-4">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Measurement</p>
    <h1 class="max-w-3xl">Measuring How Agents Fare on Your Platform</h1>
    <p class="lead text-muted mt-5 max-w-2xl">
      Six measures, the rules that make them trustworthy, and where to
      start when you have nothing yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container-prose pb-12">
    <h2>Why traditional DevRel metrics are blind</h2>
    <p class="mt-5">
      The standard DevRel dashboard tracks human participation: active
      community members, forum posts per month, event attendance, ambassador
      program NPS, documentation traffic, support ticket volume. These
      metrics measure the human community layer accurately.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      They tell you nothing about the agent ecosystem layer.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      A platform with declining forum activity may have a thriving agent
      ecosystem — developers successfully using AI tools to integrate don't
      need to post questions. A platform with excellent community metrics may
      have a catastrophically degraded agent ecosystem — developers using AI
      tools encountering systematic failures, then quietly abandoning the
      platform rather than debugging AI-generated code.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-5">
      The two layers can diverge. In practice, they do diverge. And the
      divergence is currently invisible to most DevRel programs.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>The scorecard</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        No single number describes how agents fare on a platform. The
        scorecard has six measures, one or more for each stage of the
        <a href="/what-is-agentic-relations/">agent journey</a>.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Measure</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">What it answers</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">How</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Selection rate</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">How often does an agent choose the platform for a neutral request in its category? How often does it build its own?</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">A fixed set of unbranded prompts for each model and harness. Record the first choice, the alternatives, and build-your-own.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Completion rate</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Does the agent reach a working integration?</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Two figures: with no human intervention (FAISR), and with up to a stated number of interventions. State the grading rule.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Cost</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">What did it cost, including the failures?</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Turns, tokens, and wall-clock time per run across all runs, and total spend divided by successes.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Artifact lift</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Does this skill, server, file, or documentation change serve its purpose?</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">The same evaluation with and without it, paired by task. Report the difference and its interval.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Agent reach</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Are agents arriving, and are they using what is published for them?</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Agent share of documentation requests. How often a skill or tool is loaded when it is available.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Outcome link</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Does any of this move adoption?</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">A staged change, with activation and retention compared across the two groups. See below.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 text-sm text-muted">
        Disclosure: the author does advisory work and is building a
        product in this area (see <a href="/about/">About</a>). The method
        on this page is published as an
        <a href="https://github.com/cwoodruff/agenticairelations/blob/main/docs/measurement-specification.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open specification</a> under a Creative Commons
        Attribution license, so anyone can use it without either.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>FAISR, defined</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        First-Attempt Integration Success Rate was this site's original
        headline metric. It stays, as the completion measure, with the
        word "attempt" pinned down.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-5 space-y-2">
        <li>A <strong>run</strong> is one developer request handed to one agent (a named model and harness). It ends when the agent stops, or when a stated budget of time or turns runs out.</li>
        <li>Inside a run the agent may <strong>retry on its own</strong> as often as the budget allows.</li>
        <li>A <strong>human intervention</strong> is any further message or edit from the person.</li>
      </ul>
      <Callout type="primary" label="FAISR">
        <strong>First-Attempt Integration Success Rate:</strong> the share
        of runs that pass the graders with no human intervention.
      </Callout>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Coding agents write, run, read the error, and try again, so
        counting only the first generated answer would be stricter than
        real use. Vendors who publish evaluations make the same kind of
        allowance: <a href="https://supabase.com/blog/introducing-supabase-evals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supabase</a> lets agents retry once after a
        failure before grading.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>Rules that make the numbers trustworthy</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-5 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Report the sample and the interval.</strong> If runs were independent, 20 runs at 50% would mean somewhere between about 30% and 70%, and 90 runs about 40% to 60%. Real runs repeat the same tasks, so uncertainty about tasks you did not test is wider still.</li>
        <li><strong>Say what the number generalizes to.</strong> A score describes this task suite on this model. Repetitions show how stable the agent is on those tasks. Only more, and more varied, tasks say anything about the rest.</li>
        <li><strong>Size the suite from a pilot.</strong> Run a small pilot, see how much results vary between repetitions and between tasks, then choose the numbers needed to see the difference you care about. As a starting point I would not go below about 30 tasks and three repetitions per model. That is a judgment, not a derived figure.</li>
        <li><strong>Pin and name the model and harness.</strong> Re-run on every model release. Report each model separately.</li>
        <li><strong>Publish the grading rule, strict and lenient.</strong> In <a href="https://stripe.dev/blog/stripe-integration-evals-methodology" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stripe's integration evaluations</a>, requiring every necessary criterion to pass drops the average score from 78.1% to 29.4%.</li>
        <li><strong>Automate the graders.</strong> Check the resources created and the tests passed. Hand scoring does not scale and drifts.</li>
        <li><strong>Do not benchmark competitors on a private rubric.</strong> If comparison matters, use a published task set others can run.</li>
        <li><strong>Run the suite on your own releases.</strong> A documentation or API change that lowers the score is a regression.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>Where the signal comes from</h2>

      <h3 class="mt-8">Controlled evaluation (the primary source)</h3>
      <p class="mt-4">
        Build a suite of integration tasks covering the paths developers
        take most. For each, write the median prompt — what a reasonably
        competent developer would type, not the cleverest prompt. Run each
        through each agent in scope, and grade the result:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-4 space-y-2">
        <li>Does it build and run?</li>
        <li>Does it use current endpoints? (Parse and compare against the API specification.)</li>
        <li>Does it authenticate against a test instance?</li>
        <li>Did it create the right resources and handle the primary error state?</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="mt-10">Agent traffic</h3>
      <p class="mt-4">
        Documentation hosts and content networks can now separate agent
        requests from human page loads. The agent share of your
        documentation traffic, and which pages agents fetch, tells you
        whether agents are arriving and what they read.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-10">Developer survey</h3>
      <p class="mt-4">Add two questions to your existing survey:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 mt-4 space-y-2">
        <li>"When you use AI coding tools to integrate with this platform, approximately what percentage of the time does the generated code work without significant manual correction?"</li>
        <li>"When AI-generated code for this platform doesn't work, what is the most common reason?" <em>(Options: deprecated endpoints, incorrect auth, missing error handling, over-engineered output, unclear documentation, other)</em></li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="mt-10">API logs (a hypothesis, not a method)</h3>
      <p class="mt-4">
        An earlier version of this page said AI-generated integrations
        leave recognizable patterns in API logs: failed auth on the first
        call, hits on deprecated endpoints, retries at fixed intervals.
        That has not been validated, and first integrations written by
        people fail in similar ways. Treat it as something to test
        against traffic you know came from agents.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>The outcome link</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The hypothesis is that better completion leads to more adoption.
        Comparing developers who arrive through an agent with those who
        do not is useful description, but the two groups differ in
        experience, task, and fit, so it cannot show cause.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        To test it, change one thing for part of your audience — a staged
        rollout of a documentation change or a skill — and compare
        activation and retention across the two groups, stating what else
        differed. Until that is done, call it a hypothesis.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>Agents that operate the product</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The measures above cover agents that choose and build. Agents
        also act on a platform at runtime on a user's behalf. The
        measures below are proposals. I have not seen them tested.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Proposed measure</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">What it answers</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Delegated task completion</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Of tasks an agent attempts within the scope its user granted, what share finish correctly?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Out-of-scope attempts</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">How often does an agent try something its credentials or limits should not allow, and is every attempt refused and logged?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Recovery rate</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">After an interrupted or failed operation, how often is the account left in a known, correct state?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Approvals requested</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">How often does the agent have to stop and ask a person, and for what?</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>Guardrails</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        Three things to watch so that serving agents does not cost you
        elsewhere:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mt-5 space-y-2">
        <li><strong>Maintainer load:</strong> agent-opened issues and pull requests on your open-source projects, and the time spent reviewing them.</li>
        <li><strong>Security:</strong> whether every change to agent-facing content was reviewed before release.</li>
        <li><strong>Human community health:</strong> the measures you already track.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>Starting sequence — no existing instrumentation</h2>

      <Callout type="primary" label="Step 1">
        Write the task list — 10 tasks, median prompts. Run them by hand
        against two AI tools. Score by hand. <strong>This is your
        baseline.</strong> It is too small to track changes with, and it
        is already more signal than anything in your current dashboard.
      </Callout>

      <Callout type="primary" label="Step 2">
        Automate the graders. Add repetitions and a third model. Grow the
        suite toward the size your pilot says you need.
      </Callout>

      <Callout type="primary" label="Step 3">
        Add a set of unbranded prompts to measure selection. Turn on
        agent traffic reporting for your documentation. Add the two
        survey questions.
      </Callout>

      <Callout type="primary" label="Step 4">
        Test each agent-facing artifact with and without. Run the suite
        on every release, and on every new model.
      </Callout>

      <p class="mt-6">
        The temptation is to wait until full instrumentation is in place.
        Don't. The first hand-scored run tells you something you did not
        know.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-site pt-4 pb-20">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <p class="font-semibold text-lg">
        Next:
        <a href="/for-devrel-teams/">For DevRel Teams</a>
        ·
        <a href="/for-companies/">For Companies</a>
        ·
        <a href="https://github.com/cwoodruff/agenticairelations/blob/main/docs/measurement-specification.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The open specification</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
